{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B043",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B023"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B043 remains the most complete and cleanly delimited achievement before us: the magic-wavelength lattice idea permits interrogation of many atoms while suppressing the trapping perturbation, and its experimental realization has produced extraordinarily accurate, stable, independently reproduced clocks. This is not simply an incremental improvement in timekeeping, but a durable new regime of atomic control and frequency measurement that enables tests and measurements beyond timekeeping. Katori and Ye remain the most intelligible compact attribution of the originating concept and defining experimental development. The citation should remain confined to invention and realization of optical lattice clocks, rather than claiming credit for all later architectures, comparison methods, frequency-comb work, or metrological applications.",
    "case_against": "The supplied discussion does not itself settle the historical boundary between the original magic-wavelength proposal, decisive realizations, and the many later advances required for present performance; that boundary and the Katori-Ye allocation still warrant a focused historical audit. B043 is also an enabling measurement technology, so its scientific merit should rest on the demonstrated transformation in atomic measurement rather than on future fundamental results it may make possible. B024 and B023 show that other optical instruments have equally tangible scientific and societal reach, but neither presently offers as compact a combined account of physical concept, mature implementation, and individual credit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the Aharonov-Bohm and Berry effects reveal the observable physical significance of quantum phase, while recognizing that the two effects have distinct histories.",
        "response": "I agree with the depth, broad experimental verification, and continuing influence of B021. His own qualification remains decisive for my final assessment: a citation joining those separate historical discoveries must work unusually hard to avoid making them appear as one event. B043 has one experimentally realized measurement principle and a narrower, more readily testable attribution, without diminishing the importance of quantum-phase physics."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth stresses that B043 combines the magic-wavelength concept with independently reproducible extraordinary clock performance, but cautions against absorbing later metrological advances into the prize.",
        "response": "I agree fully with that boundary. It reinforces rather than weakens the case: a prize can recognize the invention and realization of the lattice-clock regime without crediting every enabling technology or subsequent refinement. The resulting citation is precise about what Katori and Ye are being recognized for and does not overclaim the downstream physics."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson prefers aberration-corrected electron optics because it made atomic-scale structure directly observable, while noting uncertainty over the indispensable roles of Rose, Haider, and Urban.",
        "response": "B046 is a compelling example of an instrument changing what can be seen, and I share the value placed on demonstrated observational capability. Yet the unresolved question of whether two names adequately capture the corrector theory, practical realization, and related experimental development is material. Against that uncertainty, B043 presently has the more defensible two-person account, while retaining comparable maturity as a precision instrument."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a historical audit can draw a sufficiently exact line between Katori and Ye's prize-defining invention and realization and later clock architectures, frequency-comb techniques, and metrological refinements.",
      "Whether the supplied record fully establishes that the Katori-Ye pair, without additional contributors, captures every indispensable contribution to the original optical-lattice-clock breakthrough.",
      "Whether the diffuse theory-to-implementation lineage of B024 can ultimately support a more precise three-person attribution than currently shown."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent. My WACQT leadership overlaps institutionally with Göran Johansson's Chalmers environment, but he is not a proposed laureate and the supplied evidence identifies no relevant collaboration with the proposed laureates."
  }
}
